+++ b/.claude/skills/update-neurovault-app/SKILL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,206 @@
+---
+name: update-neurovault-app
+description: >
+  Update the NeuroVault APPLICATION (this code checkout) to a newer upstream
+  release WITHOUT losing the user's local add-ons (SSD eject button, auto-open
+  watcher, loading splash, mmap release fix, graph tweaks, etc.). Uses a rolling
+  one-deep backup: snapshot the current state, rebase the local add-ons onto the
+  new release, then delete the PREVIOUS update's backup. Windows-first. Invoke
+  when the user says "update NeuroVault", "update the app", "there's a new
+  version", "update to vX.Y.Z", or similar. NOTE: updates app code/binary only —
+  refreshing a project's CONTENT into its brain on the SSD is a separate
+  cloning/indexing workflow, not this skill.
+---
+
+# Update the NeuroVault app, keep my tweaks
+
+This repo is a **fork-with-local-tweaks**. `origin` is the upstream NeuroVault repo
+(`github.com/sirdath/NeuroVault`). The user works on a branch named
+`custom/v<NN>-with-local-tweaks` that carries a handful of personal commits on top
+of an upstream release tag, plus some **uncommitted** working-tree tweaks.
+
+The update model the user wants (their words): **"backup → update (passing any
+tweaks I've made) → delete the old backup."** It's a rolling one-deep safety net:
+always keep the snapshot from *this* update, delete the snapshot from the *previous*
+one.
+
+> Do the phases in order. Phases 1–2 are git surgery — show the user what you're
+> about to delete before you delete it. Phase 3 is the build gotcha that bit us
+> last time. Phase 4 proves it actually runs.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 0 — Survey (read-only, no changes)
+
+```bash
+git fetch origin --tags
+node -p "require('./package.json').version"     # CURRENT version, e.g. 0.5.2
+git branch                                       # find custom/v*-with-local-tweaks + any backup
+git tag | sort -V | grep '^v' | tail -5          # newest upstream tags
+git log origin/main..HEAD --oneline              # OUR custom commits (the ones to carry)
+git status --short                               # uncommitted local tweaks to carry too
+```
+
+Establish four facts and state them back to the user before touching anything:
+- **CURRENT_VER** — from `package.json` (e.g. `0.5.2`).
+- **TARGET_TAG** — the release to move to. Use the newest `v*` tag unless the user
+  named one. Confirm it's actually newer than CURRENT_VER.
+- **OUR_COMMITS** — `git log origin/main..HEAD` = the personal commits to replay.
+- **OLD_BACKUP** — any existing `custom/*backup*` / `custom/*working*` branch. This
+  is the one Phase 1 deletes.
+
+If `git status` shows uncommitted tweaks, they're intentional (the user hand-edits
+files like `index.html`, `src-tauri/src/lib.rs`, `src/App.tsx`). They get carried
+via stash in Phase 2 — do **not** discard them.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 1 — Backup, and rotate out the previous backup
+
+```bash
+# Snapshot the CURRENT pre-update state (commits only; uncommitted tweaks are
+# carried separately in Phase 2, but commit-state is what a revert needs).
+TODAY=$(date +%Y%m%d)
+git branch custom/v${CURRENT_VER//./}-backup-$TODAY        # e.g. custom/v052-backup-20260612
+```
+
+Then **delete the previous backup** — but show it first:
+
+```bash
+git branch | grep -iE 'backup|working'      # confirm which is OLD vs the one just made
+git branch -D <OLD_BACKUP>                   # e.g. custom/v041-backup-20260612
+```
+
+Invariant after this phase: exactly **one** backup branch exists — the one made just
+now. If the user has uncommitted tweaks they care about that are NOT yet a commit,
+mention that the backup branch only captures committed state; the working-tree
+copy is still on disk and gets carried in Phase 2.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 2 — Carry the tweaks onto the new release
+
+```bash
+git stash push -u -m "local tweaks before $TARGET_TAG"   # set aside uncommitted tweaks
+git rebase $TARGET_TAG                                     # replay OUR_COMMITS onto the new release
+```
+
+**Rebase conflict policy** — the upstream delta is large (last time: 126 files).
+When a conflict hits one of our commits, **keep our addition and re-apply upstream's
+surrounding changes around it**. Our changes are additive (a new button, a new Rust
+command, a new pragma) — they should slot in next to upstream's, not replace them.
+After resolving: `git add <files>` then `git rebase --continue`.
+
+Then restore the uncommitted tweaks:
+
+```bash
+git stash pop          # may conflict — resolve per the rules below
+```
+
+`stash pop` conflict rules (these are the exact cases we hit):
+- **Content conflict in a file we both changed** (e.g. `src/App.tsx`): keep BOTH —
+  our additions (Eject button) *and* upstream's new markup (window-mode menu). Take
+  the stashed side for our hunk, keep upstream's for theirs.
+- **modify/delete where upstream DELETED the file** (e.g.
+  `src-tauri/gen/schemas/capabilities.json` — an auto-generated artifact): accept the
+  deletion → `git rm <file>`. Don't resurrect generated files.
+- After resolving everything: `git add -A`, then `git stash drop`.
+
+Finally, rename the working branch to the new version:
+
+```bash
+git branch -m custom/v${CURRENT_VER//./}-with-local-tweaks custom/v${TARGET_TAG_DIGITS}-with-local-tweaks
+```
+
+Sanity check: `git log --oneline -6` — OUR_COMMITS should sit directly on top of the
+TARGET_TAG release commit.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 3 — Stage the sidecar (REQUIRED since v0.5.x, or the build won't even check)
+
+v0.5.x bundles `neurovault-server` as a Tauri **sidecar** (`externalBin`). The crate's
+`build.rs` validates that binary exists on **every** compile — including `cargo check`
+and `tauri dev` — so a fresh checkout fails with:
+
+```
+resource path `binaries\neurovault-server-<triple>.exe` doesn't exist
+```
+
+Stage it once (the helper does the `TAURI_CONFIG` escape-hatch build + copy):
+
+```powershell
+pwsh -File .claude/skills/update-neurovault/stage-sidecar-dev.ps1
+```
+
+Then verify the Rust side compiles:
+
+```bash
+cd src-tauri && cargo check        # expect: Finished, no errors
+```
+
+`src-tauri/binaries/` is gitignored — the staged binary is a build artifact, never
+committed.
+
+---
+
+## Phase 4 — Launch and verify it actually runs
+
+**Kill any stale `neurovault.exe` first** — a leftover process locks
+`target\debug\neurovault.exe` and the rebuild dies with `Access is denied (os error 5)`:
+
+```powershell
+Get-Process neurovault -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Stop-Process -Force
+```
+
+**Launch.** If the SSD brain is plugged in, prefer the real launcher
+`Start-NeuroVault-SSD.cmd` (it sets `NEUROVAULT_HOME` with the *quoted* `set "VAR=val"`
+form — important, see gotcha below). Launch with **separate** redirect files via
+`Start-Process` — do NOT use `cmd /c "script.cmd > log"`, the in-cmd redirect gets
+mangled and no log appears:
+
+```powershell
+$out="$env:LOCALAPPDATA\NeuroVault\devrun.log"; $err="$env:LOCALAPPDATA\NeuroVault\devrun.err.log"
+$env:NEUROVAULT_HOME="D:\NEURO-VAULT-STORAGE\.neurovault"   # only if launching npx directly
+Start-Process npx.cmd -ArgumentList "tauri","dev" `
+  -WorkingDirectory "<repo>" -RedirectStandardOutput $out -RedirectStandardError $err `
+  -WindowStyle Hidden -PassThru
+```
+
+**Verify** (all three should pass):
+- `Get-Process neurovault | Select MainWindowHandle` → non-zero (window is up).
+- `Invoke-WebRequest http://127.0.0.1:8765/api/status` → HTTP 200 (backend alive;
+  note `/status`, `/brains` etc. are 404 — `/api/status` is the real route).
+- stderr log contains `vault watcher started for brain <id>` — and `<id>` matches the
+  active brain in the SSD's `brains.json` (proves it read the SSD, not the C: fallback).
+
+---
+
+## Phase 5 — Report
+
+Tell the user:
+- New working branch: `custom/v<NEW>-with-local-tweaks` (= release + their commits).
+- Backup kept: `custom/v<CURRENT>-backup-<date>` (one revert away:
+  `git checkout custom/v<CURRENT>-backup-<date>`).
+- Old backup deleted: `<OLD_BACKUP>`.
+- Which add-ons survived (eject button/command, watcher, splash, mmap fix, graph tweaks).
+- Anything new the release adds that they'll notice.
+
+If anything failed verification, say so plainly and offer the one-line revert above —
+do **not** claim success on a build you didn't see run.
+
+---
+
+## Gotchas log (things that bit us on the v0.4.1 → v0.5.2 update)
+
+- **Trailing-space env var.** `cmd /c "... && set NEUROVAULT_HOME=D:\..\.neurovault && .."`
+  (unquoted) captures the space before `&&` *into the value* → path `…\.neurovault `
+  → "system cannot find the path" panic at `vault_dir()`. Fixed in source with
+  `.trim()` on the env var, and the launcher `.cmd` uses the quoted `set "VAR=val"`
+  form. If you ever set it inline, use the quoted form.
+- **Sidecar not staged** → build.rs check fails before any compile (Phase 3).
+- **Stale neurovault.exe** → `Access is denied` removing the exe on rebuild (Phase 4).
+- **In-cmd redirect** (`cmd /c "x > log"`) → no log file created; use `Start-Process`
+  `-RedirectStandardOutput/-Error` instead.
+- **Auto-generated files in conflicts** (`gen/schemas/capabilities.json`): accept
+  upstream's deletion, don't resurrect.
